===National Assembly===

In the National Assembly elections, the DPP won 114 seats (though the election of the vice president vacated one of those), obtaining a strong majority in the 193-seat National Assembly, while the MCP trailed distantly with 26 seats and the UDF won only 17.<ref name=Control/> Mutharika and the DPP won an overwhelming victory in northern Malawi, but also performed well in the central and southern regions, although those regions have been historically dominated by the MCP and UDF respectively. Some analysts suggested that this election marked a departure from Malawi's traditional voting patterns, which are heavily influenced by region.<ref>Felix Mponda, {{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20130124210406/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jRSnd7vZoTxq8zusgwDFkvX7pZqw|title="Malawi vote marks new era: analysts"}}, AFP, 21 May 2009.</ref> Unlike Tembo, Muluzi accepted the official results of the election.<ref name=Control/> 32 independent MPs were elected, though many of those started joining the DPP after the election; one seat was won by the [[Maravi People's Party]] (MPP), the [[Alliance for Democracy (Malawi)|Alliance for Democracy]] (Aford) and the [[Malawi Forum for Unity and Development]] (MAFUNDE). In the Blantyre City Centre constituency, the election was postponed due to the death of a candidate, a by-election was held in August 2009, which was won by an independent candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/90855/6669733.html |title=Malawi's new parliament sworn in |publisher=People's Daily Online |date=2009-06-02 |accessdate=2020-02-05}}</ref>

